<section id="about" class="editorial"><h2>About</h2><div><h3>Biography</h3><p>Katie Kratzer (b. 1985) is a contemporary painter based on Mercer Island, Washington, in the Pacific Northwest. Her practice explores the interconnected nature of existence, investigating relationships between consciousness and matter, the individual and the greater whole, the natural and spiritual worlds, and the visible and unseen.</p><p>Working primarily with acrylic and chalk pastel on raw canvas, Kratzer brings together expansive color fields, gestural mark-making and deliberate composition. Her paintings move between abstraction and, at times, subtle representation. Color is central to her visual language, with individually developed palettes informed by felt sensation, synesthetic perception and a formal education in design.</p><p>Kratzer approaches creativity as an act of receptivity. Through what she describes as the creative current, painting becomes a means of listening, receiving and giving physical form to that which is sensed, received or remembered. Her practice is as much an act of returning as it is an act of creating.</p><p>Her work draws inspiration from the natural world, quantum cosmology, mycorrhizal networks, ancient civilizations and spiritual understandings of interconnectedness. Through distinct yet interrelated bodies of work, she explores consciousness, energy, distant memory, time, unity and transformation. She is drawn to the possibility that what appears separate may, in fact, be part of a greater whole.</p><p>Kratzer earned a degree in Visual Communication Design from the University of Cincinnati’s College of Design, Architecture, Art, and Planning (DAAP). After working in design and coaching rowing, she returned to her art practice during motherhood. Her work is informed by the artistic lineage of the women of Abstract Expressionism, particularly Joan Mitchell, Helen Frankenthaler and Grace Hartigan.</p><p>Her paintings have been exhibited at the MIVAL Gallery, included in the Museum of Northwest Art’s annual auction and featured in Frankie Gaw’s cookbook <i>First Generation</i> (Ten Speed Press). Her work is held in private residential and corporate collections. She serves on the Mercer Island Arts Council and is the mother of three sons.</p><a class="link" href="#cv">View CV ↗</a><h3>Artist Statement</h3><p>My practice is an ongoing exploration of interconnectedness—the relationships between individual forms, between ourselves and the natural world, between consciousness and matter, and between the visible and unseen.</p><p>Working between intuition and intention, I use color, gesture, composition and materiality to investigate these relationships. My background in visual design informs an underlying sensitivity to structure, while the act of painting allows me to move beyond the rational and into a space of discovery.</p><p>Through distinct but interconnected bodies of work, I explore the nature of perception, energy, distant memory, unity and transformation. I am drawn to the possibility that what appears separate may, in fact, be part of a greater whole.</p></div></section>
<section id="cv" class="editorial"><h2>CV</h2><div class="cv"><h3>Exhibitions &amp; Art Auctions</h3><h4>2026</h4><p>Treasures in Miniature — MIVAL Gallery, Mercer Island, WA</p><h4>2025</h4><p>Stellar Radiance — 34th MoNA Art Auction, Museum of Northwest Art, La Conner, WA</p><p>MIVAL Holiday Show — Mercer Island Community and Events Center, Mercer Island, WA</p><p>Treasures in Miniature — MIVAL Gallery, Mercer Island, WA</p><h3>Publication</h3><p><i>First Generation</i> — Frankie Gaw, Ten Speed Press (artwork featured)</p><h3>Education</h3><p>University of Cincinnati, DAAP — Visual Communication Design, 2004–2010</p><p>Leatrice Eiseman — Master Color and Design Course, Bainbridge Island, WA, 2015</p><h3>Professional Affiliations &amp; Community</h3><p>Mercer Island Arts Council — Council Member, 2025–present</p><p>Mercer Island Visual Arts League — Member, 2025–present</p><p>Pixie Hill Preschool — President, Communications, Board Member, 2024–2026</p><h3>Design Experience</h3><p>Freelance Brand Design — Sole Proprietor, 2006–present</p><p>NBBJ — Lead Experience Designer, 2017–2018</p><p>Alaska Airlines — Senior Visual Designer, 2017</p><p>Reztark Design Studio — Director of Graphic Design, 2006–2017</p><p>Bluesky Creative — Senior Graphic Designer, 2012–2014</p><p>Vanderbyl Design — Design Co-op, 2008, 2009</p><p>National Park Service — Design Co-op, 2007, 2008</p><h3>Design Awards</h3><p>SEGD Global Design Merit Award — Alaska Airlines Hub HQ, 2017</p><p>Interior Design Magazine Best of Year Award — Healthcare Finalist, Kaiser Permanente WA</p></div></section>
<section id="studio" class="editorial"><h2>In the Studio</h2><div><p>An ongoing dialogue between spontaneous gesture and deliberate composition.</p><h3>Creative Current</h3><p>I approach creativity as an act of receptivity. Painting becomes a space for listening, receiving and allowing what already exists within to emerge. Rather than always seeking something new, I am interested in the act of returning—to intuition, to connection and to a deeper awareness of what is already present.</p><p>In the studio, I embrace curiosity, allowing myself to move deeper into the creative current without the need for a predetermined outcome.</p><p>Color is central to my practice. Each palette is individually developed, with hues mixed by hand rather than applied directly from the tube. I am drawn to the relationships between colors, their energetic qualities and their capacity to transform our perception of one another.</p><p>Through color, pigment, gesture and the physical qualities of the canvas, I give form to what emerges through this process.</p><a class="link" href="#contact">Studio inquiries ↗</a></div></section>
